from common import *
it('KERNEL-0279','decision','3.7','One geometry engine on every host (GEO-9, DEF-2; J-K4): booleans, buffers, per-edge insets and bands are built on the kernel own arrangement - the machinery of insertChain on a scratch overlay - and no second geometry engine ships (one exception: the pre-decided M0 fallback).',['§3.7 Decision','§3.1 booleans row','§3.2 (4)'],'kernel',['@insertChain'],['@booleans','@buffer','@kerb','@bandUnion','@corridorUnion','@insetRing','@offsetFace','@tileRings','§6.5 determinism'],['oneEngine','one geometry engine','J-K4','GEO-9','DEF-2'])
it('KERNEL-0280','mechanism','3.7','Boolean algorithm: overlays both operands rings, labels every face with its winding numbers (a breadth-first walk from the unbounded face adding each crossed edge ring orientation), keeps the faces the operation and fill rule select (non-zero or even-odd, declared per call), and removes the edges separating two kept faces; an empty result is E_DEGENERATE with detail empty.',['§3.7 Decision','§3.4 transient overlap labelling'],'kernel',['@area2Big (self-crossing operands)'],['@booleans','@transientArena overlap labels','@tileRings'],['windingWalk','winding numbers','empty'])
it('KERNEL-0281','mechanism','3.7','Buffer algorithm: the union of per-segment strips and per-vertex join discs: round joins as chords of at most 2 cm sagitta (about 27 chords per full circle at a 3 m radius), square ends where declared (gates, bridgeheads); where the half-width varies along a stored chain it is sampled from the chain knot profile at each segment two ends and the segment strip is the trapezoid between them (§2.2 rule 5).',['§3.7 Decision'],'kernel',['@sampleProfile','§2.2 rule 5'],['@buffer','@kerb','@bandUnion'],['2 cm sagitta','27 chords','trapezoid strip'])
it('KERNEL-0282','rule','3.7','Every engine output takes and returns integer coordinates and is produced by the same insertion rules as every other chain, so it obeys the same attraction, placement, minEdge and clearance (GEO-35) with no re-snap round trip, and on every host it is the same TypeScript code producing the same integers - no host can compute a different town.',['§3.7 Decision','§3.5 thorns row'],'kernel',['@insertChain'],['§6.5 cross-host identity'],['no re-snap round trip'])
it('KERNEL-0283','mechanism','3.7','The kerb, defined once (GEO-20): for an L1 face F, kerb(F) = F minus the union of buffer(spine_i, half-width_i) over F bounding spines, each half-width sampled from the width the spine linear entity declares - a way width profile (§2.4); on the underground layer a sewer tunnel corridor half-width = widthCm / 2 + wallCm (half its bore plus its wall; Tunnel.widthCm, II.7.2 profile row), and another system gallery = Gallery.widthCm / 2 + Gallery.wallCm (§2.7; defaults from its system row, II.7.6) - with round joins on the union convex side and square ends where declared; the union concave corners (block corners at junctions) are sharp by construction.',['§3.7 The kerb, defined once','§3.4 kerb()'],'kernel',['§2.4 way width profile','II.7.2 Tunnel.widthCm/wallCm','§2.7 Gallery.widthCm/wallCm','II.7.6 system rows','@buffer'],['@corridorUnion','§4.6 S8 cuts','§4.7 S10 frontage','II.7.3 underground plots'],['kerb','GEO-20','kerb formula'])
it('KERNEL-0284','mechanism','3.7','corridorUnion commits exactly the kerb geometry at L2: the kerb rings as kerb edges; the spine inside the band (already an L1 edge) splits it into two half-corridor strips; a corridor_split edge from each junction vertex to the kerb corner separates the junction wedge; on a side where the spine passes straight through a junction vertex (a T-junction whose street arrives from the other side) there is no kerb corner and no wedge: one corridor_split edge from the vertex to the foot of its perpendicular on that side kerb separates the half-corridor strips of the spine two sub-edges (§2.2 rule 1) - the edge II.1 (3) neighbour step inserts alone into a finished neighbour strip; the faces outside the band are the built blocks.',['§3.7 The kerb'],'kernel',['@kerb'],['§4.5 S9','II.1 (3) neighbour step','V01 neighbourhoods','§8 renderers (kerb, corridor faces)'],['half-corridor strip','junction wedge','corridor_split','built block'])
it('KERNEL-0285','rule','3.7','Kept faces inside a kerb: a finer face an earlier stage made inside F is kept (§3.3): it must lie wholly inside one resulting L2 face, and a kerb or corridor_split edge that would cross one or come within attractCm of it returns E_KEPT. The one-click order leaves none inside the faces S9 bands (a wall band, setback and glacis parts are built after S9 inside their own L1 strip faces, §4.7 S10 (0), §4.8), and a strip ends at the corridor of the way through each gate, so every gate passage stays open by construction.',['§3.7 The kerb'],'kernel',['@keptContent'],['@E_KEPT','§4.7 S10 (0)','§4.8 gates'],['gate passage open'])
it('KERNEL-0286','rule','3.7','kerb is also a read-only service on the faces of an open transaction overlay: S8 plans every cut on kerb(F) of the current L1 faces, inserts each accepted lane at L1 in its own district transaction and recurses on the children kerbs; S9 then commits corridorUnion for those L1 faces - the same function on the same inputs - so the block S8 cut and the block S9 keeps are one face (tested, §13.2). Because a way width is a knot profile, the T-junction S8 inserts into a crossed spine (§4.6 step 7) adds a vertex that carries no knot and changes no half-width, so both sides of that contract read the same widths.',['§3.7 The kerb'],'kernel',['@kerb','@sampleProfile'],['§4.6 S8','§4.5 S9','§13.2 S8/S9 same-face test'],['S8-S9 kerb contract'])
it('KERNEL-0287','rule','3.7','corridorUnion takes a set of L1 faces and re-derives only those, which is also how an edit re-derives the corridors around a changed street.',['§3.7 The kerb'],'kernel',['@corridorUnion'],['§7.3 edit re-derivation'],['local corridor re-derivation'])
it('KERNEL-0288','rule','3.7','Insets: the per-edge half-plane inset of the 2017 idea survives only as insetRing, for a uniform inset of a face whose convexity is checked exactly (E_NON_CONVEX otherwise; an inset larger than the inradius returns E_DEGENERATE with detail collapsed and the last non-empty distance); per-edge distances on any face use the buffer difference (offsetFace), so a concave, mixed-class face (the usual organic-growth face) has one correct construction, and a 2 deg kink between a 6 m and a 3 m half-width no longer lets the wider offset govern about 86 m of the narrow edge.',['§3.7 The kerb','§3.4 insetRing/offsetFace'],'kernel',['@insetRing','@offsetFace'],['@E_NON_CONVEX','@E_DEGENERATE collapsed','§4.7 setbacks'],['insetConvex','half-plane inset','collapsed','86 m'])
it('KERNEL-0289','rule','3.7','Wall bands, glacis, tower and gatehouse footprints use bandUnion with the band role: the wall chain (already an L1 edge) splits the band, so a band is a FaceRef of per-side L3 parts and never straddles its L1 edge.',['§3.7 The kerb'],'kernel',['@bandUnion'],['§4.7 S10 (0)','§4.8 walls'],['band role','per-side L3 parts'])
it('KERNEL-0290','rule','3.7','The same functions on every arena (J-A12.31): kerb, corridorUnion and bandUnion take the arena from the transaction key (§3.3) and a chain from its (role, entity) row, so they run on the sewer network part of the underground layer (part 0, §3.4) on tunnel chains as on the ground way chains; a sky island arena needs nothing of its own (its ways are ways).',['§3.7 The same functions on every arena'],'kernel',['@ArenaKey'],['II.7 sewer','V04 underground','V05 sky'],['J-A12.31','same functions every arena'])
it('KERNEL-0291','rule','3.7','Sewer part bands: corridorUnion over part 0 L1 faces commits each tunnel band (its bore plus a wall each side) as its two half-corridor strips with a junction wedge at every tunnel junction, and the faces between bands as that part built blocks (level mapping §2.2 rule 1; which blocks are plotted and how deep, II.7.3); the band outer ring, the tunnel external wall, is the kerb edge every underground frontage plot lines end on (§4.7 S10 frontage-first cut on that kerb), so a plot stands flush against the wall by construction (item 12; frontage rule and invariants II.7.3 U1-U3 and §5.3).',['§3.7 The same functions on every arena'],'kernel (plotting rules owned by II.7.3 / V04)',['@corridorUnion','II.7.3'],['II.7.3 U1-U3','§5.3','V04 underground plots'],['tunnel band','flush against wall'])
it('KERNEL-0292','rule','3.7','One cut for every underground part (item 65; J-A65.32): on the other systems parts corridorUnion commits each gallery or walkway band from the half-width its Gallery record declares (as for a tunnel); faces between the bands are that part blocks (which are plotted: II.7.5, II.7.6, as II.7.3 decides for the sewer). A CHAMBER - a void wider than a band (a cavern hall, a quarry chamber, an undercity main chamber) - is a closed chain carrying the structural chamber_wall role (§2.2 rule 7), inserted by insertChain (closed) at the part L1 (§2.2 rule 1 underground mapping), so the chamber floor and the rock around it are separate L1 faces; its outline declares no half-width (as a shore), so its own edge is the kerb a plot on the floor may end on; a walkway across the floor is a gallery chain banded by corridorUnion; S9-S11 then cut the floor faces as a ground L1 face. A place where two parts meet is a record, never a band (§3.4). No arena-specific band operation exists.',['§3.7 One cut for every underground part'],'kernel (systems owned by II.7.5-II.7.6 / V04 underground)',['§2.7 Gallery','§2.2 rule 7 chamber_wall','§2.2 rule 1 underground mapping'],['II.7.5','II.7.6','V04 underground'],['chamber','chamber_wall','J-A65.32'])
it('KERNEL-0293','decision','3.7','Oracles, not fallbacks (J-K19): Clipper2 (compiled to WebAssembly, run in Node) and polygon-clipping are dev dependencies of @plat/testkit only; no product bundle loads either unless the M0 speed fallback fires. Because our engine attracts features closer than attractCm and the oracles do not, B-FUZZ splits every boolean and buffer case by what our engine reports.',['§3.7 Oracles, not fallbacks'],'kernel (test harness §13.1)',['@plat/testkit (§14)'],['§13.1 B-FUZZ','§14 package deps'],['oracle','B-FUZZ','J-K19','@plat/testkit'])
it('KERNEL-0294','test','3.7','B-FUZZ unattracted cases: the operation reports no W_ATTRACTED, W_STRETCH_SHARED, W_WEDGE_ROUTED or W_PLACED (nothing moved beyond the hot pixel): our result and each oracle must have equal face counts after both are snapped (zero-area faces dropped) and a symmetric-difference area of at most perimeter x 1 cm, the perimeter the summed boundary length of both operands.',['§3.7 Oracles bullet 1'],'kernel (bar) / §13.1 (harness)',['W_ATTRACTED','W_STRETCH_SHARED','W_WEDGE_ROUTED','W_PLACED'],['§13.1 B-FUZZ'],['unattracted bar','perimeter x 1 cm'])
it('KERNEL-0295','test','3.7','B-FUZZ attracted cases (thin, near-collinear and near-touching inputs §13.1 random family and constructive set include): our result must pass every hard invariant of §3.10, and its symmetric difference from each oracle must be at most perimeter x sqrt(2) * attractCm (about 36.8 cm), the farthest any boundary point can move under attraction, placement or wedge routing (a wedge-routed tip sweeps at most half a minEdge per unit of arm length); face counts not compared (merging features closer than attractCm is the designed difference).',['§3.7 Oracles bullet 2'],'kernel (bar) / §13.1 (harness)',['@invariants'],['§13.1 B-FUZZ'],['attracted bar','36.8 cm'])
it('KERNEL-0296','rule','3.7','The harness draw count (keeping the strict comparison from being vacuous) is §13.1. There is no runtime fallback, so no host produces a different model hash and no fallback path needs a budget (SCL-22). The generation report names the engine that ran - the arrangement engine, plat-arrangement@<version> (§5.4); an engine change: §6.5 (7).',['§3.7 after oracle bullets'],'kernel',[],['§5.4 generation report engine name','§6.5 (7) engine change','§13.1 draw count'],['plat-arrangement@<version>','SCL-22'])
it('KERNEL-0297','test','3.7','M0 (a) confirmation: (i) differential correctness on 10^6 fuzzed and constructive inputs per operation - 0 crashes, 0 invalid outputs, and the split oracle agreement; (ii) speed: S9 corridor bands over the CITY face set against §12.4 S9 row and one district <= 3 ms on reference hardware, and S11 geometry share against its §12.4 row on B-CITY-lite S11 workload. S11 geometry share = every kernel call S11 makes for one plot (base outline builder frontage-band intersection or OBB/LIR fit (§2.6, §4.7), per-edge setbacks by offsetFace, the yard partition splits and the footprint insertion into the block L4 batch) - the geometry part of S11 single §12.4 row, whose remainder is S11 rule evaluation (designation template, heights, materials, entrance), so this bar cannot pass while the stage misses its row. Criterion (i) has no fallback: a failure is a kernel bug fixed before M1.',['§3.7 Confirmation at M0'],'kernel (milestone §16 M0; budgets §12.4)',['§12.4 S9, S11 rows','B-CITY-lite'],['§16 M0 exit','§12.4'],['M0 (a)','10^6 inputs','3 ms per district','S11 geometry share'])
it('KERNEL-0298','fallback','3.7','M0 speed fallback: if (ii) fails after one optimisation pass, the shipped engine becomes Clipper2 compiled to WebAssembly as the only engine on every host: outputs still enter the arena through insertChain (attraction, placement, minEdge, clearance hold); WebAssembly becomes a stated requirement of the embed contract (§11); a host that cannot instantiate it receives §11.2 E_ENGINE_UNAVAILABLE - never a different town; the switch is a declared golden shift whose shift records name every stage reading a boolean, buffer, inset or band (S1 outline repair, S3 reservations, S8 kerbs, S9 corridors, S10 step (0) wall and margin bands, S11 footprints, S12b greenery faces). Clipper2 does not reproduce the arrangement engine integers, so §6.5 (10) cross-implementation byte-identity test applies to the WebAssembly accelerator only, and Clipper2 determinism check is §6.5 (9)-(10) cross-host digest identity.',['§3.7 Confirmation at M0'],'kernel',['@oneEngine'],['§11 embed contract','§11.2 E_ENGINE_UNAVAILABLE','§6.5 (9)-(10)','§13 golden shift records'],['Clipper2 fallback','E_ENGINE_UNAVAILABLE','declared golden shift'])
it('KERNEL-0299','decision','3.7','No WASM kernel (DEF-13; J-K8): the kernel is TypeScript on every host. M0 (a) measures its per-op costs against §12.4 rows - insert + snap + attract + re-walk per growth proposal (S5), splitFace per bent cut (S8), and the band and S11 costs.',['§3.7 No WASM kernel','§3.1 skeleton row (no WASM kernel)'],'kernel',['§12.4 rows'],['§16 M0'],['no WASM kernel','DEF-13','J-K8'])
it('KERNEL-0300','fallback','3.7','What an M0 per-op miss triggers, per hot loop after one optimisation pass: (a) miss by more than 2x: that loop only is compiled from the same integer algorithm into a WebAssembly accelerator behind the kernel facade; (b) miss by up to 2x: the measured cost is carried into its stage row of §12.4; if that pushes the row over budget the accelerator is built as above, otherwise the measured cost replaces the row figure as a declared budget amendment (recorded in §12.4 and the M0 record, critical path re-summed); (c) an accelerated loop that still misses, or a re-summed critical path above its rung p95 (§12.3), re-declares the model budget at the measured p95 as a D-18 amendment for the owner veto - the same path the fusion gate uses (§16).',['§3.7 No WASM kernel bullets'],'kernel',['§12.3 p95','§12.4 rows'],['§12.4 budget amendment','D-18 amendment','§16 fusion gate'],['accelerator','2x rule','declared budget amendment'])
it('KERNEL-0301','rule','3.7','The WebAssembly accelerator must pass §6.5 (10) cross-implementation test byte-identically on the golden suite (§16 M0 exit names this rule), and the TypeScript loop stays in every bundle, so a host without WebAssembly is slower and never different.',['§3.7 after bullets'],'kernel',['@accelerator'],['§6.5 (10)','§16 M0 exit'],['byte-identical accelerator'])
add(I)
